  "name": "Stan Getz",
  "editorial_article": "Stan Getz (born Stanley Gayetski; February 2, 1927 – June 6, 1991) was an influential American jazz saxophonist known for his distinctive warm, lyrical tone, which earned him the nickname 'The Sound.' A virtuoso on the tenor saxophone, Getz rose to fame in the late 1940s as a member of Woody Herman's Second Herd, where his playing in the 'Four Brothers' saxophone section became a hallmark of the era's jazz sound. Influenced heavily by the melodic and wispy style of Lester Young, Getz was a versatile musician who moved fluidly between the rigorous improvisations of bebop and the relaxed sensibilities of cool jazz.\n\nHis career was marked by profound artistic shifts, most notably his pivotal role in introducing bossa nova to American audiences. In the early 1960s, Getz collaborated with Brazilian giants João Gilberto and Antônio Carlos Jobim. Their 1964 collaboration, *Getz/Gilberto*, produced the global smash hit 'The Girl from Ipanema' and cemented Getz's status as a crossover star. Throughout his long and storied career, Getz recorded for major labels including Prestige, Verve, and Columbia, consistently producing work that balanced technical precision with deeply expressive, melodic improvisation. His ability to blend mainstream jazz accessibility with profound emotional depth made him one of the most celebrated and imitated tenor saxophonists of the 20th century. Throughout his five-decade career, Getz remained a restless explorer of sound, moving from his early bebop roots in the mid-1940s to the breezy, sophisticated cool jazz of the 1950s, and eventually finding the definitive global fusion sound with his bossa nova output. His legacy is etched in dozens of classic albums and a style that is instantly recognizable for its poise and clarity. Always a formidable technician, Getz managed to avoid the common pitfalls of showmanship, preferring instead to craft long, winding solos that felt both inevitable and surprising. Whether leading his own quartets or featuring in major ensemble projects, Stan Getz remained a cornerstone of jazz music until his death in 1991.",
